{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And for all the mighty hand</b> — the splitting of the Sea of Reeds, as it is written concerning it (Exodus 14:31), \"and Israel saw the great hand.\" <b>And for all the great awe</b> — the standing at Mount Sinai, as it is written concerning it (Exodus 20:20), \"in order that His fear may be upon your faces.\" And therefore he mentioned here that these were done \"before the eyes of all Israel,\" for he had already mentioned all that was done to Pharaoh and to all his servants and to all his land. And in the Sifrei (at its end): \"<b>And for all the mighty hand</b>\" — this is the plague of the firstborn, as it is written concerning it (Exodus 6:1), \"for with a strong hand He shall send them out.\" \"<b>And for all the great awe</b>\" — this is the splitting of the Sea of Reeds. Another interpretation: \"<b>and for all the great awe</b>\" — this is the giving of the Torah. And by way of truth, <b>the mighty hand</b> is the attribute of judgment, as in the expression \"the hand of HaShem was upon me\" (Ezekiel 37:1; ibid. 40:3), and likewise \"for the hand of HaShem has done this\" (Isaiah 41:20), and therefore they say in the legal context (Ruth 1:13), \"for the hand of HaShem has gone out against me.\" <b>And the great awe</b> is the attribute of mercy, as in the matter that is written (Isaiah 8:13), \"Him shall you sanctify, and He is your awe.\" And to this the Sifrei alludes in the plague of the firstborn and in the splitting of the Sea of Reeds, for it is written concerning them (Isaiah 63:14), \"to make for Yourself a name of glory.\"" "And the sense of <b>which Moses did</b> is that he prepared and displayed these before the eyes of all the people, in the manner of \"and the souls that they had acquired in Haran\" (Genesis 12:5), \"and he hastened to make it\" (Genesis 18:7), \"to make the day of the Sabbath\" (Deuteronomy 5:15) — for Moses did not make the mighty hand and the great awe, but rather he prepared them, and on his account they were performed before the eyes of all Israel." "These five books are completed, in which the wisdom of Agur and Ithiel fell short, and thousands of blessings and thanksgivings to El, who has dealt with me according to His mercies and the multitude of His kindnesses, and has brought what my heart asks, and it is for the sake of His name Yoel, and the Redeemer shall come to Zion, speedily in our days Michael shall stand, to build the House and the Ariel, and may He fulfill the verse written through Ezekiel (37:28), \"and the nations shall know that I, HaShem, sanctify Israel, when My sanctuary is in their midst forever.\" And blessed be HaShem, the Elohim of Israel, from the world and unto the world." "I found in a Midrash on Proverbs: At the hour the Sanhedrin sought to appoint Solomon among the three kings and four commoners, the Shekhinah stood before the Holy One, blessed be He, and said: Master of the Universe, have You seen \"a man swift in his work\" and so forth (Proverbs 22:29). And in our Gemara in chapter Ḥelek, \"the likeness of his father's image came and prostrated itself before them\" and so forth. And in the Jerusalem Talmud, \"David came and prostrated himself before them.\" And all of them intended one and the same thing — to say that the Holy One, blessed be He, goes and comes from attribute to attribute: He goes out from the attribute of judgment and comes to the attribute of mercy, which is the attribute of David, as they said concerning the sanctification of the new moon: \"David, king of Israel, lives and endures.\" And the enlightened will understand."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {0 "The dual citation (Ezekiel 37:1 and 40:3) is collapsed in the Hebrew as (יחזקאל לז א שם מ א); rendered literally.", 1 "The idiom לָעֲשׂוֹת meaning 'to prepare/make ready' is illustrated by three biblical comparanda where עשה carries a similarly preparatory or causative sense rather than literal fashioning.", 2 "The passage is Ramban's poetic colophon; several phrases embed names or allusions (Agur, Ithiel, Yoel, Michael, Ariel) whose identification is deliberately cryptic. Rendered hyper-literally without expansion.", 3 "The reference is to the rabbinic debate (b. Sanhedrin 104b; y. Sanhedrin 10:2) over whether Solomon is among those who have no share in the world to come, and the heavenly interventions on his behalf."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:00:56.804989274Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}